1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,812
The brush clears a smooth stone, covered with small, neat letters. For centuries no one could read them.

2
00:00:07,812 --> 00:00:18,453
Then, in 1837, a scholar solved the puzzle. These are the edicts of Ashoka. Who first read these letters in modern times?

3
00:00:35,283 --> 00:00:39,119
How do we know? Inscriptions are the key evidence.

4
00:00:39,119 --> 00:00:44,520
They are carved on rocks and pillars, and they are the oldest deciphered royal writing in India.

5
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,720
Nothing needs to be dug up, because the words are in the open.

6
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:09,770
Ashoka's inscriptions are found from Afghanistan to Karnataka. They were written in Prakrit.

7
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:13,089
Most used the Brahmi script, read left to right.

8
00:01:13,089 --> 00:01:19,324
In the north-west, at Shahbazgarhi and Mansehra, the script is Kharosthi, read right to left.

9
00:01:19,324 --> 00:01:25,779
In Afghanistan, edicts have been found in Greek and Aramaic. There are several types.

10
00:01:25,779 --> 00:01:31,650
The Major Rock Edicts are fourteen long texts. The Minor Rock Edicts are shorter.

11
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:37,353
The Pillar Edicts are seven texts on tall pillars, such as those at Delhi-Topra and Allahabad.

12
00:01:37,353 --> 00:01:43,081
Some edicts are cut in caves, such as at the Barabar hills. A few are personal.

13
00:01:43,081 --> 00:01:46,964
The Queen's Edict names Karuvaki, mother of Tivara.

14
00:01:46,964 --> 00:01:55,957
In 1837, James Prinsep, an officer of the East India Company, read the Brahmi script. The key was a Delhi pillar.

15
00:01:55,957 --> 00:02:02,630
The edicts speak in a clear voice: this is what I did, and this is what I ask my people to do.

16
00:02:17,983 --> 00:02:26,741
Old name, new name. The word is dhamma in Ashoka's Prakrit, and dharma in Sanskrit. Both words mean the right way to live.

17
00:02:26,741 --> 00:02:30,691
Ashoka's edicts always use the Prakrit form.

18
00:02:46,017 --> 00:02:57,468
Carve this tip into memory. Brahmi left, Kharosthi right. Prinsep read it in 1837. Two scripts, one decipherer and a year.

19
00:02:57,468 --> 00:03:00,035
Those are the facts to remember.

20
00:03:15,383 --> 00:03:31,929
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which script is read from left to right in most edicts? The answer: Brahmi. Number two.

21
00:03:31,929 --> 00:03:50,456
Who deciphered Brahmi in 1837? The answer: James Prinsep. Number three. In which hills are cave inscriptions of Ashoka found?

22
00:03:50,456 --> 00:04:00,970
The answer: the Barabar hills. Now one line for Mains. Assess the value of Ashoka’s edicts as a historical source.

23
00:04:20,317 --> 00:04:28,995
The edict tablet goes into its case. It carries a king's voice across twenty-two centuries. Three facts to take away.

24
00:04:28,995 --> 00:04:42,515
Brahmi, Kharosthi, Greek and Aramaic. Major and minor, rock and pillar. Brahmi read by Prinsep in 1837. Next gallery: Mauryan Art.

